## Migrating from `grafana_folder`

`grafana_folder` uses Grafana's legacy folder API, which has no concept of folder
ownership, so team folders are only available through this resource.

There is no in-place upgrade: the two are different resource types backed by
different APIs. But both are keyed on the folder UID, so a folder can be handed
over without being recreated.

### Attribute mapping

| `grafana_folder` | `grafana_apps_folder_folder_v1` |
|---|---|
| `uid` | `metadata.uid` |
| `title` | `spec.title` |
| `parent_folder_uid` | `metadata.folder_uid` |
| `url` (read-only) | `metadata.url` (read-only) |
| `prevent_destroy_if_not_empty` | no equivalent — use `lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }` |
| `org_id` | no equivalent — see [Organizations](#organizations) below |
| — | `spec.description` (new) |
| — | `metadata.owner_references` (new — team folders) |

### Handing a folder over

Order matters: `import` needs the new resource block to already exist in the configuration, and
`removed` needs the old block to be gone. Make all the edits below before running
`terraform plan` — do not plan or apply with both resource blocks present, or Terraform will try
to create a second folder with the same UID.

**1. Replace the resource block.** Swap the `grafana_folder` block for its equivalent, using the
attribute mapping above:

```terraform
# Remove this
resource "grafana_folder" "example" {
  uid   = "my-folder-uid"
  title = "My Folder"
}

# Add this
resource "grafana_apps_folder_folder_v1" "example" {
  metadata {
    uid = "my-folder-uid"
  }

  spec {
    title = "My Folder"
  }
}
```

**2. Update anything that references the folder**, as described in
[Updating references](#updating-references) below.

**3. Move the folder across in state.** On Terraform 1.7 or later, add these blocks:

```terraform
removed {
  from = grafana_folder.example

  lifecycle {
    # Keep the folder in Grafana; only remove it from Terraform state.
    destroy = false
  }
}

import {
  to = grafana_apps_folder_folder_v1.example
  id = "my-folder-uid"
}
```

On earlier versions, skip the blocks and do the same thing imperatively once steps 1 and 2 are in
place:

```shell
terraform state rm grafana_folder.example
terraform import grafana_apps_folder_folder_v1.example my-folder-uid
```

**4. Check the plan, then apply.** What matters is that **no folder is being destroyed or
replaced** — not that the plan is empty. In-place changes are expected here, since the first
apply also writes Terraform's manager annotation (see below).

Once the apply succeeds, delete the `removed` and `import` blocks.

### Updating references

Anything pointing at the old folder needs its expression updated, from
`grafana_folder.example.uid` to
`grafana_apps_folder_folder_v1.example.metadata.uid`. Consumers such as
`grafana_folder_permission_item`, `grafana_dashboard.folder` and
`grafana_rule_group.folder_uid` all take a plain UID string, so only the
expression changes — not the value.

### Organizations

`grafana_folder` takes a per-resource `org_id`. This resource does not: App
Platform resources derive their namespace from the provider's `org_id` (or
`stack_id` on Grafana Cloud). To manage folders across several organizations,
use a separate aliased provider per organization.

### UI edits after migrating

Importing only reads the folder, so nothing about it changes in Grafana at that point. The next
apply that writes the folder marks it as managed by Terraform, and from then on it cannot be
edited in the Grafana UI. If that matters — folder titles are a common case — set
`options { allow_ui_updates = true }`, accepting that the configuration and Grafana can then
diverge.

